Class Scheduler step by step teacher manual
Shared Compass Academy • Grade 11 • Mac • Browser ChatGPT
Q2W2 through Q4W8
Begin with the existing README-only repository. Follow the steps in this manual in order to build the application gradually with the class and finish the required product by Q4W8. The teacher uses ChatGPT in a browser, saves its output into VS Code, runs checks on the Mac, and records each reviewed change in Git.
One document in teaching order
This manual incorporates the first-week prompt pack and the technical instructions that previously appeared separately. Each week contains preparation, Monday through Wednesday lessons, and Thursday and Friday student work with the teacher key immediately afterward. Each lesson prints its own prompt, commands, checks, and completion steps. The old Q2W2 prompt pack is no longer needed.
What the finished product includes
Rooms, teachers, courses and sections; manual scheduling for one active term using Monday–Friday and P1–P3; server checks for room, teacher, section, and capacity conflicts; Microsoft sign-in with Admin and Teacher permissions; stable approved timetable publishing; an Azure teaching deployment; and an always-on VPS deployment with an adult operations owner. Student enrollment and individual student schedules are outside the scope.
What students do
Students help define tasks, improve prompts, make small guided changes, review a peer’s work, test supplied cases, and explain the result. The teacher operates AI on classroom days. Home practice uses short guided browser or paper examples. No advanced independent app building is assigned at home.
Use actual observations
The prompts below are ready to copy. Where a real account ID, hostname, approved budget, or observed error cannot be known in advance, the immediately preceding steps explain how to obtain it and provide it as evidence. Do not replace these facts with invented example values. A check is passed only after the actual result is observed.
Preserve grades and submissions from the six previously taught foundation weeks. Those weeks did not build this app. All required product work in this manual fits within the remaining 25 instructional weeks; later refinement is limited to nonblocking improvements.
Read once before the first lesson
Class time and preparation
Each Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday class is 60 minutes: 0–12 explain and predict; 12–17 choose the task and improve the prompt; 17–40 generate, save, and check the current increment; 40–55 guided student contribution and review; 55–60 record evidence and close. Account/tool preparation may occur before class. Application functionality is built during its scheduled lesson, not prebuilt offstage.
The aide keeps students on the supplied example while the teacher diagnoses a problem. If the current task needs more time, complete its repair, show the diff next class, and keep future features unbuilt. Q4 includes repair and verification capacity. Required identity, hosting, and publication work cannot be moved beyond Q4W8.
Grading and home routine
Use the existing gradebook. Recommended remaining-work weights: classroom process/contributions 40%; guided home practice 25%; basic-concept checkpoints 20%; milestones/reflection 15%. Preserve already-earned grades. Score understanding and evidence rather than independent advanced coding.
Home tasks take 20–30 minutes each day. Stop at 30 minutes. A labeled paper sketch and the same short answers earn equal credit if a website or device fails. Work is due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday; after that, apply the established one-letter-grade reduction per week late. Publish the final submission date on the school calendar.
Weekly evidence rubric, 10 points: basic explanation 2; supplied task or paper equivalent 2; expected/actual comparison 2; prompt/task/review contribution 2; clear reflection 2. Checkpoints are one point per basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language and provide a short correction opportunity.
Adult access that must be ready on time
Before Q2W2: Mac tools, ChatGPT, and repository access. Before Q2W5: Python. Before Q2W6: Docker. Before Q3W2: approved Microsoft tenant administrator and test identities. Before Q3W8: Azure subscription, supported plan/network permissions, registry, and budget owner. Before Q4W1: approved VPS, domain/DNS access, adult SSH owner, backup owner, and off-host backup destination. The steps for each appear in that week.
Source and command conventions
A repository-root terminal starts in the folder containing README.md. In VS Code Explorer, right-click that top-level folder and select Open in Integrated Terminal. Use a fresh root terminal for each printed block unless it explicitly continues a running session. Mac editing uses Command; stop a server with Control+C. Run command lines individually unless shown as one multi-line script. Copy a complete script as one block; visual wrapping does not create new command lines.
Use VS Code for file creation, diffs, staging, commits, branches, and uploads. A Command Palette action is a menu choice: press Command+Shift+P, type its name, and select it; do not enter it in Terminal. Publish Branch uploads a new branch to the existing repository. Push uploads later commits. Sync Changes both pulls and pushes, so use the separate Pull and Push actions below. The source-snapshot helper only reads Git metadata; app, database, and hosting commands still run in the integrated terminal.
Prepare the Mac before Q2W2
1 Install or verify VS Code and Git
Download VS Code for macOS https://code.visualstudio.com/download
Download the matching Mac version, open the download, and move Visual Studio Code into Applications. Open it. Open the Mac Terminal application and run git --version. If macOS offers Command Line Tools, complete that installation, then run the command again. It must report a Git version before continuing.
git --version
2 Install or verify Node and npm
Official Node download https://nodejs.org/en/download
Select the latest available Node 22 maintenance release and the macOS installer. This guide requires at least 22.12 in the Node 22 line for its React 19/Vite 7 baseline. Open the downloaded .pkg and finish installation. Quit and reopen VS Code, then open Terminal > New Terminal and run:
node --version
npm --version
Expect v22.x with a minor version of 12 or higher, and an npm version. If a different Node version appears, resolve the installation/PATH before class. Do not install an entire app starter.
3 Open the existing repository
In the repository’s browser page, copy its HTTPS clone URL from the Code menu. In VS Code press Command+Shift+P, choose Git: Clone, paste that URL, select a local parent folder, and open the cloned folder. If it is already cloned, use File > Open Folder and select that existing folder; do not create a second repository.
1. In Explorer, confirm README.md is the only project file. Click Source Control and confirm Changes and Staged Changes are empty.
2. Read the current branch in the bottom-left status bar. In the repository browser, identify the default branch and keep that exact name for the lessons.
3. In VS Code select File > Open File. Press Command+Shift+. in the Mac file chooser to show hidden items; open this repository’s .git/config. Check that [remote "origin"] has the intended repository URL, then close it without changing the remote.
4. In Source Control, expand Graph to inspect the latest commit. If hidden, open the Source Control … menu > Views > Source Control Graph.
Use your authorized repository account when VS Code opens a browser sign-in. Keep credentials out of the remote URL and out of anything shared with ChatGPT. Save this repository folder as the classroom working copy; use File > Open Recent to return to it.
4 Verify commit identity
In VS Code choose File > Open File and open this repository’s .git/config (Command+Shift+. shows hidden files in the chooser). Find the [user] section. Verify its name and email match your approved commit identity. If absent, add the section at the end. Enter your actual name and approved Git email after the equals signs, using this layout:
Replace both descriptive values with your real approved identity before saving. Preserve every existing setting; if [user] exists, edit its fields rather than adding a duplicate section. Save with Command+S and close the file. This sets identity only for this repository; signing in to GitHub is separate. Do not add .git/config as a project file.
[user]
    name = Your actual name
    email = Your approved Git email
5. Install the GitHub Pull Requests and Issues extension: click Extensions in the left Activity Bar, search GitHub.vscode-pull-request-github, select the GitHub-published extension, and click Install. Click its GitHub icon, select Sign In, and finish the browser prompts. This adds pull request review and merge controls; basic file editing and Git uploads are built into VS Code.
Create the ChatGPT Project before Q2W2
1. Open ChatGPT in the browser and select New project. Name it Class Scheduler classroom build. Open the project’s three-dot menu, choose Project settings, and paste the complete instructions below into its instructions field. Save them.
Help me teach Grade 11 students to build Class Scheduler gradually over 25 instructional weeks from Q2W2 through Q4W8. I am the teacher, using a Mac, ChatGPT in a browser, VS Code, and Git. I manually save and run your output. Our repository initially contains only README.md. Students help plan, improve prompts, make small guided edits, review, test, and commit in class. They do not independently build advanced features at home.
Required final product: rooms, teachers, courses, sections, manual scheduling in one active term with Monday through Friday and fixed periods P1, P2, P3; server conflict and capacity rules; Microsoft sign-in with Admin and Teacher roles; immutable approved timetable publication; Azure teaching deployment; permanent VPS deployment. No student enrollment or individual student schedules. Finish required work by Q4W8.
Implement only the current lesson. Do not prebuild future models, routes, screens, flags, or hidden features. Use React 19 with Vite 7 and plain JavaScript; Python 3.12 with FastAPI; PostgreSQL 16 when its week arrives. These are deliberate course versions, not claims about the newest releases. Preserve the actual existing files and contracts. Never reset working data or remove volumes to hide an error.
I will attach a current source snapshot and observed output. Treat those as authoritative; ask for a specific missing fact instead of inventing it. First list the affected paths. Then provide complete new or revised file contents, one clearly labeled file per code block, without ellipses. Use VS Code GUI steps for file creation, review, staging, commits, branches, fetch, pull, push, and pull requests. Give terminal commands only for app/tool setup, running, testing, database, hosting, or the provided source-snapshot helper, with the directory for each. Keep source edits separate from commands. For large files, split the response at file boundaries and wait for Continue; do not omit unchanged portions of a replacement file. Explain the important changed lines in plain language. Never claim that you ran my app or that a check passed unless I supply the result.
Introduce dependencies only when needed. Keep .env, tokens, passwords, keys, dumps, node_modules, dist, virtual environments, and temporary logs out of Git and chat. Public client IDs are not secrets. No browser client secret is allowed. Keep application data fictional while developing. Every lesson ends with a small check and a truthful evidence note. Future work belongs on the task board, not in implementation.
2. Start a chat inside that Project named Q2W2 Monday. Attach the existing README.md using the attachment control. Do not attach a generated scheduler or the full future manual as a request to implement everything.
3. Keep VS Code and ChatGPT side by side. ChatGPT in the browser does not automatically edit the repository. You will save each named file, run the commands, inspect the result, and commit the change yourself.
ChatGPT Project instructions https://help.openai.com/en/articles/10169521-projects-in-chatgpt
Q2W2 Weekly preparation
Plan the product and create its first page
Starting point: Only the existing README and Git history exist.
End-of-week check: A scoped project board, a minimal React page, and reviewed student commits.
Explain these ideas before the build
A repository stores the source and its history; it is not a running application. A requirement describes an observable user outcome. An acceptance check says how we will recognize success. A prompt needs current state, one requested change, constraints, and a check. React displays the first page; Vite runs the development server. A package file lists dependencies, while the lockfile records resolved versions. Nothing beyond a heading and purpose sentence is implemented this week.
requirement: A clear statement of what a user needs.
prompt: The instructions and context given to AI.
commit: A recorded group of source changes in Git.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Verify VS Code, Git, Node 22.12 or later within the Node 22 line, and npm. Open the README-only repository. Prepare a blank task card and projector. Confirm the school owner can provide Microsoft test identities, Azure access, and a VPS/domain by the later milestone dates. Do not create app files before class.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Create Backlog, Ready, Doing, Review, and Done columns in docs/board.md. Limit Doing to one class build task. Name an owner and one acceptance check for each card.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Improve Build my app into a bounded request for one page. Students identify the missing audience, current files, scope, and success check.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
