Use the supplied manual checks today. If the request above mentions tests, provide manual expected/actual cases only; the automated pytest framework is introduced in Q3W1.

Today return only docs/product-brief.md, docs/board.md, and docs/prompt-log.md. Put the first React page in Ready. Include Backlog, Ready, Doing, Review, Done, and Blocked headings. A small source-snapshot helper is added separately by the teacher after these documents; it is not app functionality.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
Create docs/product-brief.md, docs/board.md, and docs/prompt-log.md beside the existing README directory. Save the reviewed contents. Then create scripts/classroom-context.sh and paste the entire helper below. This reads current files into a temporary text attachment; it implements no application feature.
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
{
  printf '%s %s current source\n' "${1:-Current}" "${2:-lesson}"
  git status --short
  git log -1 --oneline
  git ls-files
  git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -- \
    README.md 'docs/*.md' 'frontend/*.json' \
    'frontend/*.html' 'frontend/*.js' 'frontend/src/*' \
    'backend/*.py' 'backend/*.txt' 'backend/alembic.ini' \
    compose.yaml Dockerfile .dockerignore .gitignore \
    'deploy/*.yaml' deploy/Caddyfile '.github/workflows/*' \
    | sort -u | while IFS= read -r course_file; do
      printf '\nFILE %s\n' "$course_file"
      cat "$course_file"
      printf '\n'
    done
} > /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
printf '%s\n' 'Created /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt'


Run the helper once to check it. Do not upload .env files or private credentials:
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q2W2 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
6 Run the check and handle a failure here
1. Confirm README is unchanged. Open each of the three docs files; the board must list the first page as Ready and later features as Backlog.
In a separate repository-root terminal, run the checks below. Run only against the isolated local test configuration when pytest is included. A stopped service or missing dependency must be resolved before recording a pass.
In Source Control, click every changed file and read its diff. Confirm only the planned documents/helper changed and no unexpected file is listed. Open each saved file in Explorer to check its text.
If a check fails: copy the exact command, the first useful error, what you expected, and what appeared into the same ChatGPT chat. Remove credentials. Then paste this ready-to-use repair request:
The check failed. The actual command, expected result, observed result, and first error are pasted immediately above. Use today's source snapshot and the files we changed in this chat. Identify the failing layer and give one small diagnostic first. Then give the smallest correction, complete contents for any changed file, and the exact Mac recheck command. Preserve working data and unrelated behavior. Do not regenerate the entire app or implement future tasks.
Save only the correction it returns, rerun the failed check, and repeat relevant earlier checks. If no failure occurred, skip the repair prompt. Keep the aide’s paper exercise moving while the teacher diagnoses.
7 Guide the student contribution
Pairs improve one vague requirement, then add one task card on a student branch. The aide checks the wording before a commit.
Use the aide-supervised classroom Mac for the reference workflow so students do not need a separate advanced environment. Rotate driver and reviewer. The driver makes only the approved small edit or writes the evidence note; the reviewer compares the diff and runs the supplied check. Record each student’s name/pseudonym and contribution in docs/student-work/. The teacher controls account authentication and the final push.
If students use approved accounts on separate computers, their PR targets lesson/q2w2-mon until that class branch is merged. First, the teacher saves, reviews, stages, and clicks Commit, then Publish Branch (or … > Push). Students use Source Control > … > Fetch and the branch picker to open that published class branch; if already on it, use … > Pull. Students must receive today’s reviewed files before editing.
After the student contribution, repeat the visible acceptance check and run these commands before committing:
In Source Control, click every changed file and read its diff. Confirm only the planned documents/helper changed and no unexpected file is listed. Open each saved file in Explorer to check its text.
8 Record and save the reviewed result
In the same ChatGPT chat, paste a short factual observation: what you did, what appeared, and the actual build/test result. Then paste this request. Save the note as docs/lesson-notes/Q2W2-Mon.md and the revised board at its existing path.
Write docs/lesson-notes/Q2W2-Mon.md for Q2W2 Monday, task Agree on the first task. Use only the observations I have just supplied to write today's lesson note. Include the task, the prompt improvement students made, paths changed, expected and actual results, pass/fail/not run, and the student contribution. Do not invent a successful check. Also provide the smallest change to docs/board.md that moves only verified work to Done; keep failures Blocked with a next action. Return complete contents for the named lesson-note file and revised board. Do not change application code.
In VS Code choose File > Save All. Click Source Control, then each file under Changes to inspect its diff. Click the + beside only each intended source, document, migration, or lockfile. Keep .env, virtual environments, node_modules, dist, dumps, and tokens out of Staged Changes. Then complete these steps:
1. In Source Control, click each file under Staged Changes to review the exact saved changes. If you edit again, save and stage that file again.
2. In the Message box, enter: Q2W2 Monday Agree on the first task
3. Click Commit. If asked to stage everything, cancel and stage only the intended files with +.
4. Click Publish Branch for the first upload and choose origin if asked. If already published, click Source Control > … > Push. Confirm the branch is lesson/q2w2-mon and wait for the upload to finish.
5. Press Command+Shift+P and select GitHub Pull Requests: Create Pull Request. Set the base to the actual default branch and compare to lesson/q2w2-mon. Enter the lesson title and observed check results, then click Create.
6. Open Changes in Pull Request and review each diff with a student. In the PR Description, confirm required checks and approvals, then use the available Merge action and confirm. If blocked, keep the PR a draft/unmerged. If nothing changed, skip the empty commit and PR.
7. After GitHub confirms Merged, exit Review Mode if it is active. Click the bottom-left branch picker and select the default branch.
8. Click Source Control > … > Fetch, then … > Pull. Confirm the merged files appear in Explorer and there are no uncommitted changes or unexpected outgoing commits. Leave this updated default branch ready for the next lesson.
The next lesson begins from this merged checkpoint. If branch protection requires another approved reviewer, complete that review before proceeding. Do not force-push to bypass it.
9 Close the lesson
Ask: Does a task on a board mean the app feature already works?
Teacher answer: No. It describes intended work; a verified result is needed.
