Q2W3 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML tables https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_tables.asp
Stop point: First Try it Yourself example only. Put the supplied words/numbers into existing cells first, then make the requested change. Do not build a new table.
Read this worked example first
<tr><td>Room 101</td><td>24</td></tr>
Change 24 to 20. The capacity cell changes while the room-name cell stays the same.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Open the first HTML table example. Change two existing cells to Room 101 and Art Studio. Run it and identify one row.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Change a supplied capacity value from 24 to 20. Record which cell changed and name one thing that stayed the same.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain requirement in your own words.
2. Which is the result a user sees: App.jsx source text or the browser page?
3. Explain component in your own words.
4. What lets the same RoomCard show a different room?
5. Can a code review correctly conclude that no change is needed?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
A row groups related values. Credit the changed cell and accurate observation, without requiring new table code.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. A clear statement of what a user needs.
2. The browser page.
3. A reusable piece of a screen.
4. The room prop passed into it.
5. Yes, if the current code already satisfies the task.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q2W4 Weekly preparation
Add a local room form and filter
Starting point: The page displays a read-only source-defined room list.
End-of-week check: A filter and validated add-room form update browser state only.
Explain these ideas before the build
State lets a screen respond to input. Filtering selects visible records without deleting them. A controlled input keeps its displayed value in React state. Adding a room changes this browser session only; a refresh resets it until persistence is built later. Basic validation rejects a blank name and a nonpositive or noninteger capacity. Client validation helps the user but will not replace server checks when an API is added.
state: A value the screen remembers while it runs.
input: Information a user enters.
validation: Checking whether input follows a rule.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Prepare Art, xyz, a blank name, and capacities 20, 0, and 2.5. Keep the current two-room list available; no form or filter has been prepared.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Define separate acceptance checks for filter and add-room behavior. Students decide what is in scope for a local prototype.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Specify boundaries: input, valid example, invalid example, and the fact that refresh resets local changes.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
