Q2W5 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML forms https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_forms.asp
Stop point: First form example only. Identify its labels, inputs, and submit control; use fictional values.
Read this worked example first
<label>Name <input type="text" value="Alex"></label>
Name is the label; Alex is the current input value. The user can change the value without changing the label.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Open the first HTML form example. Identify a label, input, and submit button. Use fictional example values only.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. On paper label I ask for rooms as request and Here are the rooms as response. Draw one helpful message for a service that is unavailable.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain state in your own words.
2. Should a room with capacity 0 be accepted?
3. Explain request in your own words.
4. Can browser-only validation stop every bad API request?
5. Does a stopped API mean the React source file was deleted?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Request asks and response replies. Credit labeled controls and a useful wait/retry explanation; no API access is needed at home.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. A value the screen remembers while it runs.
2. No. Capacity must be a positive whole number.
3. A message asking a server to do something.
4. No. The server must also validate requests.
5. No. The screen and API are separate parts.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q2W6 Weekly preparation
Add persistent room storage
Starting point: React uses the temporary-memory API; a restart loses added rooms.
End-of-week check: Room data is stored in PostgreSQL and survives an API restart.
Explain these ideas before the build
PostgreSQL persists records independently of a running Python process. SQLModel maps models to database operations; a session groups interactions and commit saves an accepted write. A Docker volume retains database files. The local Python API connects to 127.0.0.1:5432; later containers will use service DNS instead. Do not keep a memory fallback after database conversion: an unavailable database must produce an honest failure. Repeated startup must preserve existing rows.
table: Records organized with the same fields.
row: One record in a table.
ID: A value identifying one record.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Verify Docker Desktop. Prepare the database-only Compose procedure and two-row drawing. Do not prepare application database code in advance.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Record a dependency: database readiness blocks persistence tests. Define Done using a saved marker that survives restart.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Describe a migration from current behavior to new behavior, with preservation and failure requirements.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
