Q2W6 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
SQLBolt lesson 1 https://sqlbolt.com/lesson/select_queries_introduction
Stop point: Run the two supplied SELECT examples only. Do not continue to later lessons or install a database.
Read this worked example first
SELECT title FROM movies;
This requests the title column. One returned row describes one movie. Adding year after a comma requests a second column.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Use SQLBolt Lesson 1 only. Run SELECT title FROM movies; and identify one column and one row in the result.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Run SELECT title, year FROM movies; using the supplied text. State what changed compared with one column. Stop after this example.
2. Spend five minutes reviewing the example, 10–15 minutes on the task, and five minutes recording the result.
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Both queries read data. The second selects two columns. A row represents one movie; no local database setup is required.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q2W7 Weekly preparation
Build teachers courses and class sections
Starting point: Only rooms can be created and stored.
End-of-week check: Administrators can create and edit catalog records for rooms, teachers, courses, and sections locally.
Explain these ideas before the build
A course names a subject; a section identifies one taught group with a teacher and planned seat limit. A relationship stores an ID rather than copying a name. Foreign keys protect references, but application rules still check meaningful values. Schema changes now use reviewed migrations. Existing rooms must survive adding new tables. Editing is needed for a usable product; deleting referenced records must be rejected or deliberately archived rather than silently breaking meetings.
course: A subject such as Algebra.
section: One offered group of a course.
relationship: A link between records using an ID.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Prepare T1 Alex Example, T2 Jordan Example, Algebra, Art, Section A with limit 24, and Section B with limit 18. Rehearse migration vocabulary, not future feature code.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Use a dependency order: teacher/course records before sections. Split forms and routes into small cards.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: List allowed model fields and explicitly exclude enrollment and scheduling until their week.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
