Q2W9 Tuesday Check capacity and related edits
Target: 24 in 24 passes; 24 in 18 fails; lowering a used room below its section limit leaves the original capacity unchanged.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q2w9-tue and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q2W9 Tuesday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
Finder highlights the generated text file. Open and review it for accidental secrets before upload. In the Class Scheduler ChatGPT Project, start a chat named Q2W9 Tuesday. Attach this current text file. It contains actual source paths and the current commit, so you do not need to fill out a context form. Use this newest snapshot, not an older upload.
3 Explain and choose the small task
An edit elsewhere can make an existing schedule invalid. Ask students to predict this result: Can a 24-seat section use a room with capacity 24?
Read the target above. Put only this task in Doing on docs/board.md once that file exists. Ask students to identify one useful constraint in the prompt and record their wording improvement without expanding scope.
4 Send the complete lesson prompt
We are doing Q2W9 Tuesday: Check capacity and related edits. I use a Mac and ChatGPT in a browser; I will copy your files into VS Code myself. Use the attached current source snapshot. At the start of this week: The local planner can save meetings but does not yet prevent collisions. The actual snapshot takes precedence if a prior repair changed the file layout.

Today's request: Enforce section seat_limit <= room capacity for every meeting. Revalidate affected meetings on room capacity edits and section seat-limit or teacher edits. Reject changes that create capacity or teacher collisions, preserving the old data. Reuse the conflict service and add equal-capacity, one-over, and related-edit tests. No enrollment logic or new scheduling features.

Use these current file locations where they apply: backend/services.py for shared scheduling validation, backend/main.py, backend/models.py; frontend error handling; current tests. Create only files needed for today's task. Preserve existing paths unless a specific change is necessary. Return complete contents for each changed file, one labeled code block per path, and Mac run/test commands with working directories. Use VS Code GUI actions for Git operations. Do not use placeholders for facts already in the snapshot. If an account-specific value is missing, ask for that value before generating dependent configuration.

Our acceptance check is: 24 in 24 passes; 24 in 18 fails; lowering a used room below its section limit leaves the original capacity unchanged. Explain the expected observation and one relevant failure case. Do not report a pass until I provide actual output. Do not implement any later week's feature.

Use the supplied manual checks today. If the request above mentions tests, provide manual expected/actual cases only; the automated pytest framework is introduced in Q3W1.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
