Q2W9 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML input types https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_input_types.asp
Stop point: Find Input Type Number and open that example only. Stop after using the existing number control.
Read this worked example first
<label>Seats <input type="number" min="1" value="20"></label>
For the class rule, 20 is valid because it is a positive whole number. Zero is invalid. A browser control alone is not the app’s server rule.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Revisit the Number input example. Use the supplied room capacities 18 and 24. On paper decide whether a class of 20 fits each.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
Complete the ten short questions below using class notes. This replaces other Friday practice. Spend about 20 minutes; submit your answers with Thursday’s evidence.
1. What existed in the repository before Q2W2?
2. Give one detail that makes an AI prompt clearer.
3. What does a Git commit record?
4. What does a reusable component help us do?
5. Does a filter delete records that are not shown?
6. Which asks for data: a request or a response?
7. Where should a saved room remain after an API restart?
8. How is a section different from a course?
9. Can a class with a seat limit of 24 use a room with capacity 18?
10. What should happen if two classes need the same room at the same time?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
20 fits 24 but not 18. Grade the simple review using the following teacher key.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. README.md only; no application code.
2. Current files/state, one change, constraints, or an acceptance check.
3. A reviewed group of source changes in repository history.
4. Display a repeated piece of UI with different supplied data.
5. No. It changes what is displayed.
6. A request.
7. In the database.
8. A course is a subject; a section is one offered group of that course.
9. No; it exceeds capacity.
10. The conflicting placement should be rejected without changing the valid meeting.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q3W1 Weekly preparation
Make saves reliable and automate rule checks
Starting point: Sequential scheduling rules work; simultaneous edits remain unverified.
End-of-week check: Transactional writes and PostgreSQL tests protect scheduling integrity.
Explain these ideas before the build
Two requests can both see an empty slot before either saves. For this small single-term app, serialize scheduling-affecting changes by locking the existing active Term row at the start of a transaction. Then read fresh relevant data, validate, write, and commit. All paths that affect meetings, section teacher/limit, or room capacity must use the same boundary. Database foreign keys and useful uniqueness constraints provide additional protection. A failed operation rolls back. Students learn the one-at-a-time analogy; the teacher and AI implement the mechanism.
transaction: A group of changes that succeeds together or leaves the previous state.
regression: A previously working behavior that a change breaks.
evidence: An observed result supporting a claim.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Create a separate PostgreSQL test database. Confirm tests cannot point at the working class database. Prepare two simultaneous booking cards.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Turn repeated manual checks into a small regression checklist. Mark pass, fail, or not run; never infer a pass.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Ask for a failing case first, then the smallest repair and a repeated check.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
