With the school tenant administrator, open the school’s Microsoft Entra admin center and select the approved tenant. Use its actual cloud endpoints; do not assume the teacher’s unrelated workplace cloud. Open Identity > Applications > App registrations. Record public values in docs/identity-public.md as you complete these steps.
1. Select New registration for Class Scheduler API. Use accounts in this organizational directory only. Register, then record its Application client ID and Directory tenant ID from Overview.
2. Open Expose an API, set its Application ID URI, and add a delegated scope named access_as_user. Record the full scope URI shown by the portal. Have the tenant owner approve the consent policy.
3. Create a second single-tenant registration named Class Scheduler SPA. In Authentication add the Single-page application platform and redirect URI http://localhost:5173. The development app must be opened at that exact localhost address when signing in.
4. In the SPA registration, open API permissions > Add a permission > My APIs. Select Class Scheduler API and its delegated access_as_user scope. Have the authorized administrator grant consent where required.
5. Record the SPA client ID, tenant-specific authority URL, expected access-token version, API audience, scope URI, and redirect URI in the public settings file. Ask the tenant owner to verify these values. Do not create a SPA client secret.
Attach the public settings file together with today’s source snapshot. If an ID is missing, stop the dependent configuration until the owner supplies it. Students use identity/permission cards while the teacher handles the portal.
