Q3W4 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML paragraphs https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_paragraphs.asp
Stop point: First Try it Yourself example only. Change the text inside one <p>...</p>; preserve the tags.
Read this worked example first
<p>Our class is planning a tool.</p>
Change planning to building. The displayed sentence changes; the paragraph tags stay the same.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Use the paragraph editor to display Approved timetable version 1. Run it and save a screenshot or sketch.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. On paper write a separate Draft version 2 with one changed room. Circle the version staff should use until version 2 is approved.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain permission in your own words.
2. Should a Teacher see an unfinished draft merely because no timetable has been published?
3. Explain draft in your own words.
4. Is saving a draft the same as publishing it?
5. Which timetable should staff use before a new publication succeeds?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Staff use approved version 1 until a new successful publication. The saved screenshot models the stable copy.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. An action a person is allowed to perform.
2. No. Show that nothing is published yet.
3. The working timetable administrators edit.
4. No. Publishing is a separate approved action.
5. The current approved published version.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q3W5 Weekly preparation
Build the published teacher timetable
Starting point: Publication snapshots exist; the teacher endpoint still needs its actual timetable output.
End-of-week check: Each teacher reads and prints only their own approved timetable.
Explain these ideas before the build
The API filters snapshot meetings by the authenticated Teacher mapping. Do not download all teachers' data and hide rows in the browser. Sort by numeric day and period order, not alphabetical labels. Show version/time, useful empty messages, and a print layout. Admin can inspect an approved timetable through authorized routes. Keep publication history immutable and never print a draft as though it were approved.
view: The information shown for a particular task.
order: The sequence in which records are displayed.
label: Text explaining a field or status.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Prepare two teacher accounts with different published meetings, including Monday P1, Monday P2, and Tuesday P1.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Use a user journey: sign in, see own timetable, identify the version, print. Each step gets a check.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Specify the user, authorized data source, and visible result rather than asking for a generic dashboard.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
