3 Explain and choose the small task
Packaging must preserve the behavior already tested. Ask students to predict this result: Is an image the same thing as a running container?
Read the target above. Put only this task in Doing on docs/board.md once that file exists. Ask students to identify one useful constraint in the prompt and record their wording improvement without expanding scope.
4 Send the complete lesson prompt
We are doing Q3W7 Monday: Build the production image. I use a Mac and ChatGPT in a browser; I will copy your files into VS Code myself. Use the attached current source snapshot. At the start of this week: The application works locally with separate development processes. The actual snapshot takes precedence if a prior repair changed the file layout.

Today's request: Package our existing React/FastAPI app in one production image using a multi-stage Dockerfile and .dockerignore. Build the frontend with its lockfile, serve static assets through the backend, preserve /api routes, and run without a development reloader. Keep secrets outside the image and run as a non-root user where practical. Do not rebuild the product or add features. Explain the exact build context and command.

Use these current file locations where they apply: Dockerfile, .dockerignore, deploy/compose.local-prod.yaml, backend/main.py and current frontend build configuration. Create only files needed for today's task. Preserve existing paths unless a specific change is necessary. Return complete contents for each changed file, one labeled code block per path, and Mac run/test commands with working directories. Use VS Code GUI actions for Git operations. Do not use placeholders for facts already in the snapshot. If an account-specific value is missing, ask for that value before generating dependent configuration.

Our acceptance check is: Image builds from current source; its contents exclude local secrets and unnecessary development files. Explain the expected observation and one relevant failure case. Do not report a pass until I provide actual output. Do not implement any later week's feature.

Set the production working directory to /app/backend, retain python -m alembic availability, and use port 8000. Static UI and API share one origin; health paths and /api routes must precede the SPA fallback. Use committed dependency files. The teacher builds linux/amd64 from the Mac for the chosen hosting target.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
Build for the hosting architecture
A Mac may use ARM while Azure App Service uses an AMD64 Linux image. Build the same source for linux/amd64 now so this mismatch is discovered before hosting. Commit the reviewed production files first, then run the following from the repository root. The tag identifies the committed source.
In VS Code Source Control, inspect and stage only today’s intended Dockerfile, .dockerignore, and application/configuration changes with the + beside each file. Leave all environment secrets untracked. Then follow this GUI commit and terminal build sequence:
In Source Control, review Staged Changes. In Message, enter Q3W7 production packaging checkpoint, then click Commit. Open Graph, right-click this new commit, and choose Copy Commit ID. Use that copied ID when the next terminal block asks for the image tag.
Open a root integrated terminal. Run the first line below, paste the copied commit ID, and press Enter. Then run the two Docker commands in that same terminal. The image tag identifies the exact committed source.
read -r course_image_tag
docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 --load -t "class-scheduler:$course_image_tag" .
docker image inspect "class-scheduler:$course_image_tag" --format "{{.Architecture}}"
Expect amd64. If source changed after that commit, make a new reviewed commit and rebuild with its new tag. Do not claim an old commit represents uncommitted fixes. Keep the VPS architecture compatible with this image or explicitly build and test its matching platform later.
Docker platform builds https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-platform/
6 Run the check and handle a failure here
1. Build the production image from current source. Inspect .dockerignore for .env, .venv, node_modules, dumps, and unrelated files.
In a separate repository-root terminal, run the checks below. Run only against the isolated local test configuration when pytest is included. A stopped service or missing dependency must be resolved before recording a pass.
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
If a check fails: copy the exact command, the first useful error, what you expected, and what appeared into the same ChatGPT chat. Remove credentials. Then paste this ready-to-use repair request:
The check failed. The actual command, expected result, observed result, and first error are pasted immediately above. Use today's source snapshot and the files we changed in this chat. Identify the failing layer and give one small diagnostic first. Then give the smallest correction, complete contents for any changed file, and the exact Mac recheck command. Preserve working data and unrelated behavior. Do not regenerate the entire app or implement future tasks.
Save only the correction it returns, rerun the failed check, and repeat relevant earlier checks. If no failure occurred, skip the repair prompt. Keep the aide’s paper exercise moving while the teacher diagnoses.
