Q3W7 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
What is a container? https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/
Stop point: Read the introductory explanation and key properties only. Do not install or run Docker at home.
Read this worked example first
Source files → image → running container
An image is the package used to start a container. A stopped container is not proof that saved database records were deleted.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Read only the Docker What is a container explanation. Stop before Try it out. Draw an image as reusable packing instructions and a container as one running instance.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Use a lunchbox analogy: instructions/package versus one prepared lunchbox. Write one similarity and one limit of the analogy.
2. Spend five minutes reviewing the example, 10–15 minutes on the task, and five minutes recording the result.
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Credit reusable package versus running instance. No Docker commands or installations are home tasks.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q3W8 Weekly preparation
Deploy the same application in Azure
Starting point: A tested production image exists; no Azure deployment has been completed.
End-of-week check: The authenticated application runs in Azure with managed PostgreSQL and verified HTTPS access.
Explain these ideas before the build
Use one Azure teaching environment: Linux App Service running the production container and Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server. Keep the database reachable through an approved private network path from the app and configure DNS correctly. Store credentials in server settings. Use the actual school subscription, region, and supported plan; do not infer government-cloud endpoints from unrelated work. The browser requires the actual HTTPS redirect URI registered with Microsoft. Deployment is complete only after hosted workflow checks.
host: A computer or service that runs an application.
HTTPS: A protected web connection.
cloud: Remote computing services reached over a network.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. School-approved Azure subscription, spending owner, registry, suitable App Service plan, network permissions, and PostgreSQL configuration must be available. Use the Azure steps printed in this week. Keep real credentials off the projector.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Track hosting dependencies and costs before deployment. The teacher owns spending and access; students maintain the checklist.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Supply verified target details and ask for exact environment-specific steps; never invent subscription IDs or prices.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
Q3W8 Monday Provision the teaching environment
Target: The selected services and private app-to-database path are configured; unresolved access/network issues are recorded explicitly.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q3w8-mon and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
Collect Azure settings and provision in order
The teacher and school owner use the approved Azure portal. Record existing public inputs in docs/azure-public.md: cloud, subscription name/ID, approved resource group and region, proposed registry/App Service/database names, hosting mode and plan, container port 8000, network/subnet plan, image tag, and budget owner. For a registry login server, database FQDN, or app URL that does not exist yet, write not created yet. Obtain those returned values from each resource’s Overview page after creation; do not guess them. Never paste a password or credential-bearing connection string into ChatGPT.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q3W8 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
Finder highlights the generated text file. Open and review it for accidental secrets before upload. In the Class Scheduler ChatGPT Project, start a chat named Q3W8 Monday. Attach this current text file. It contains actual source paths and the current commit, so you do not need to fill out a context form. Use this newest snapshot, not an older upload.
3 Explain and choose the small task
Hosting includes connected services, not just uploading files. Ask students to predict this result: Does cloud hosting mean the application no longer runs on computers?
Read the target above. Put only this task in Doing on docs/board.md once that file exists. Ask students to identify one useful constraint in the prompt and record their wording improvement without expanding scope.
4 Send the complete lesson prompt
We are doing Q3W8 Monday: Provision the teaching environment. I use a Mac and ChatGPT in a browser; I will copy your files into VS Code myself. Use the attached current source snapshot. At the start of this week: A tested production image exists; no Azure deployment has been completed. The actual snapshot takes precedence if a prior repair changed the file layout.

Today's request: Using these verified Azure choices, guide creation/configuration of our existing app's teaching environment: Linux App Service custom container plus PostgreSQL Flexible Server with an approved private network/DNS path. Use actual portal/CLI options supported by the selected plan and region. Keep database credentials in server settings. List dependencies and verification steps. Do not invent prices, resource IDs, access, or completed deployment results.

Use these current file locations where they apply: the current production image and deploy/ configuration; docs/azure-deployment.md; existing identity settings. Create only files needed for today's task. Preserve existing paths unless a specific change is necessary. Return complete contents for each changed file, one labeled code block per path, and Mac run/test commands with working directories. Use VS Code GUI actions for Git operations. Do not use placeholders for facts already in the snapshot. If an account-specific value is missing, ask for that value before generating dependent configuration.

Our acceptance check is: The selected services and private app-to-database path are configured; unresolved access/network issues are recorded explicitly. Explain the expected observation and one relevant failure case. Do not report a pass until I provide actual output. Do not implement any later week's feature.

Use docs/azure-public.md. Guide creation in order from approved names, cloud, plan, region, and hosting mode. Ask only for inputs required for the next action. A resource URL or FQDN marked not created yet is an output to collect from its Overview page after creation, not a prerequisite for creating it. Explain exactly where to record each returned value before dependent configuration. Verify registry managed-identity pull access and private database DNS/network connectivity. Never request secrets or invent resource identifiers.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
Collect Azure settings and provision in order
1. Confirm the approved plan supports custom Linux containers and required VNet integration. Confirm current cost and school budget before creating resources. Use the school’s actual names and region.
2. Create or select the container registry and the network. Create separate appropriately delegated subnets for PostgreSQL private networking and App Service integration; connect the PostgreSQL private DNS zone to the network.
3. Create PostgreSQL Flexible Server in the approved private network configuration. Record its FQDN. Keep its credentials in the school’s approved private location.
4. Create Linux App Service for the tested container. Configure its managed identity/registry pull permission, VNet integration, target port 8000, HTTPS-only access, and /health/ready health path. Hosting modes differ: use the actual sidecar target-port field or classic custom-container port setting, not both indiscriminately.
5. Configure the server database connection with required TLS and identity-validation values. Confirm the app’s network resolves the private database FQDN. Resource creation does not prove the connection works.
Attach azure-public.md to the daily chat. Its prompt requests exact remaining portal/CLI actions using these actual values. Unknown tenant/resource values cannot be prefilled responsibly; this page tells you where to obtain each one.
Azure custom container configuration https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/configure-custom-container
6 Run the check and handle a failure here
1. Confirm App Service can resolve and reach the private database; resource creation alone is not success.
In a separate repository-root terminal, run the checks below. Run only against the isolated local test configuration when pytest is included. A stopped service or missing dependency must be resolved before recording a pass.
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
If a check fails: copy the exact command, the first useful error, what you expected, and what appeared into the same ChatGPT chat. Remove credentials. Then paste this ready-to-use repair request:
The check failed. The actual command, expected result, observed result, and first error are pasted immediately above. Use today's source snapshot and the files we changed in this chat. Identify the failing layer and give one small diagnostic first. Then give the smallest correction, complete contents for any changed file, and the exact Mac recheck command. Preserve working data and unrelated behavior. Do not regenerate the entire app or implement future tasks.
Save only the correction it returns, rerun the failed check, and repeat relevant earlier checks. If no failure occurred, skip the repair prompt. Keep the aide’s paper exercise moving while the teacher diagnoses.
7 Guide the student contribution
Students match app, database, registry, and owner to simple responsibility cards and update the deployment task board.
Use the aide-supervised classroom Mac for the reference workflow so students do not need a separate advanced environment. Rotate driver and reviewer. The driver makes only the approved small edit or writes the evidence note; the reviewer compares the diff and runs the supplied check. Record each student’s name/pseudonym and contribution in docs/student-work/. The teacher controls account authentication and the final push.
If students use approved accounts on separate computers, their PR targets lesson/q3w8-mon until that class branch is merged. First, the teacher saves, reviews, stages, and clicks Commit, then Publish Branch (or … > Push). Students use Source Control > … > Fetch and the branch picker to open that published class branch; if already on it, use … > Pull. Students must receive today’s reviewed files before editing.
After the student contribution, repeat the visible acceptance check and run these commands before committing:
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
8 Record and save the reviewed result
In the same ChatGPT chat, paste a short factual observation: what you did, what appeared, and the actual build/test result. Then paste this request. Save the note as docs/lesson-notes/Q3W8-Mon.md and the revised board at its existing path.
Write docs/lesson-notes/Q3W8-Mon.md for Q3W8 Monday, task Provision the teaching environment. Use only the observations I have just supplied to write today's lesson note. Include the task, the prompt improvement students made, paths changed, expected and actual results, pass/fail/not run, and the student contribution. Do not invent a successful check. Also provide the smallest change to docs/board.md that moves only verified work to Done; keep failures Blocked with a next action. Return complete contents for the named lesson-note file and revised board. Do not change application code.
In VS Code choose File > Save All. Click Source Control, then each file under Changes to inspect its diff. Click the + beside only each intended source, document, migration, or lockfile. Keep .env, virtual environments, node_modules, dist, dumps, and tokens out of Staged Changes. Then complete these steps:
1. In Source Control, click each file under Staged Changes to review the exact saved changes. If you edit again, save and stage that file again.
2. In the Message box, enter: Q3W8 Monday Provision the teaching environment
3. Click Commit. If asked to stage everything, cancel and stage only the intended files with +.
4. Click Publish Branch for the first upload and choose origin if asked. If already published, click Source Control > … > Push. Confirm the branch is lesson/q3w8-mon and wait for the upload to finish.
5. Press Command+Shift+P and select GitHub Pull Requests: Create Pull Request. Set the base to the actual default branch and compare to lesson/q3w8-mon. Enter the lesson title and observed check results, then click Create.
6. Open Changes in Pull Request and review each diff with a student. In the PR Description, confirm required checks and approvals, then use the available Merge action and confirm. If blocked, keep the PR a draft/unmerged. If nothing changed, skip the empty commit and PR.
