Q3W8 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML links https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_links.asp
Stop point: First Try it Yourself link example only. Change visible link text; keep its example destination.
Read this worked example first
<a href="https://example.com">Example page</a>
Example page is the visible text. The href value is the destination. Changing the text does not change the destination.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Use the first HTML link example. Change only the visible link text to Open school timetable, leaving the sample destination unchanged.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Identify the visible text and destination address. On paper label a laptop app local and a school website hosted.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain image in your own words.
2. Should the database password be bundled into browser JavaScript?
3. Explain host in your own words.
4. Will a localhost redirect automatically work for the hosted web address?
5. Does seeing the home page prove the whole hosted workflow works?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Link text and destination are different; local versus hosted identifies where the app runs. No cloud account work.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. A packaged set of files and instructions for running software.
2. No. It belongs on the server.
3. A computer or service that runs an application.
4. No. The hosted redirect must be configured correctly.
5. No. Sign-in, rules, saved data, and publication need their own checks.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q3W9 Weekly preparation
Verify Azure recovery costs and the second milestone
Starting point: Azure runs the core product, but recovery and cost procedures need evidence.
End-of-week check: The Azure demonstration is documented, recoverable, and ready to inform VPS deployment.
Explain these ideas before the build
A backup file is not proof of recovery until restored to a separate target and checked. Use a supported PostgreSQL dump/restore process or verified managed recovery flow. Keep source backups separate from database backups. Cloud budgets notify; they do not automatically stop all spending. Stopping an app can leave plan, database, or storage charges. The class records an adult-owned schedule and cleanup plan while the permanent VPS work begins next quarter.
backup: A separate copy kept for recovery.
restore: Recovering data from a saved copy.
cost: Money required to operate a service.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Have an approved separate restore target and access to cost/resource views. Do not restore over the working teaching database.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Review the milestone against required features and record operational risks with named adult owners.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Ask for an evidence-based runbook: exact action, expected result, failure response, and owner.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
