Stop point: Use only the ordered or unordered list example named in the task. Change item text, not the structure.
Read this worked example first
<ol><li>Choose</li><li>Save</li><li>Check</li></ol>
Each li is an item. Changing Save to Save draft changes that step’s words; the order stays the same.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Use the ordered-list example to show Save a copy, Keep it separate, Check the copy. Run it and describe one everyday example.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
Complete the ten short questions below using class notes. This replaces other Friday practice. Spend about 20 minutes; submit your answers with Thursday’s evidence.
1. Why repeat a check after changing code?
2. What is the difference between identity and permission?
3. May a Teacher edit the draft timetable?
4. Should a Teacher receive another teacher’s rows and merely hide them?
5. Does changing a draft immediately change the approved timetable?
6. What should happen to the current approved version if publishing fails?
7. What does a code reviewer compare?
8. How is an image different from a container?
9. Does creating an Azure resource prove the app works there?
10. How do we check that a backup is useful for recovery?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Credit a separate recoverable copy and the need to test it. Use the milestone answer key.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. To detect a regression or confirm working behavior remains.
2. Identity tells who a person is; permission controls what they may do.
3. No. That is an Admin action.
4. No. The server returns only authorized rows.
5. No. A new successful publication is required.
6. It stays active and unchanged.
7. The task, proposed changes, and verification evidence.
8. The image is a package; a container is an instance running from it.
9. No. Test actual hosted workflows.
10. Restore it into an isolated database and compare known records.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q4W1 Weekly preparation
Prepare the permanent hosting environment
Starting point: The complete current app runs on Azure; backup and restore have been demonstrated.
End-of-week check: A school-owned VPS, domain, access plan, and reviewed deployment task are ready.
Explain these ideas before the build
A deployed application needs an owner as well as code. DNS points a school-controlled name to the server. HTTPS protects browser traffic. The VPS will run the same application image developed in class, with configuration suited to this host. PostgreSQL stays private. Preparing accounts, keys, billing, and a domain is an adult logistics task; it does not mean writing app features ahead of lessons.
VPS: A rented server managed by its owner.
DNS: The system that connects a name to an address.
TLS: The protection used by HTTPS connections.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Confirm the approved VPS and domain are available. The teacher or IT owner controls payment, SSH keys, DNS, and recovery. Use the VPS steps printed in this week; keep credentials out of projected screens.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Assign one adult owner, one backup owner, and a due date to each hosting dependency. Treat missing access as a blocker, not a student homework task.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Give AI verified environment facts and ask it to identify missing inputs before suggesting commands. Never let it invent a domain or credential.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
Q4W1 Monday Map the deployment
Target: Every account and operating task has an adult owner; no invented hostnames, secrets, or completed claims appear.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q4w1-mon and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
Record the permanent hosting inputs
Open the approved VPS provider account with the adult owner. Record public values in docs/vps-public.md: provider, actual server name/IP, supported Ubuntu LTS version, CPU architecture, domain, SSH user, deploy directory, registry/image reference, backup destination description, and primary/backup owner. Use an amd64 VPS for the image tested in Q3W7, or build and test a matching image deliberately.
Obtain the server’s approved SSH access from its owner. Keep private keys/passwords in approved private storage. A school-controlled domain and DNS editor access are needed before HTTPS setup. These are adult responsibilities, not student homework.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q4W1 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
Finder highlights the generated text file. Open and review it for accidental secrets before upload. In the Class Scheduler ChatGPT Project, start a chat named Q4W1 Monday. Attach this current text file. It contains actual source paths and the current commit, so you do not need to fill out a context form. Use this newest snapshot, not an older upload.
3 Explain and choose the small task
Compare the hostname to an address label and DNS to an address directory. Ask students to predict this result: Does a domain name itself run the application?
Read the target above. Put only this task in Doing on docs/board.md once that file exists. Ask students to identify one useful constraint in the prompt and record their wording improvement without expanding scope.
4 Send the complete lesson prompt
We are doing Q4W1 Monday: Map the deployment. I use a Mac and ChatGPT in a browser; I will copy your files into VS Code myself. Use the attached current source snapshot. At the start of this week: The complete current app runs on Azure; backup and restore have been demonstrated. The actual snapshot takes precedence if a prior repair changed the file layout.

Today's request: Using our actual Azure-tested application and these verified VPS facts, draft docs/vps-plan.md. Include browser to Caddy to app to private PostgreSQL, ownership, DNS, HTTPS, backups, and acceptance checks. Refer to secrets by variable name only. List missing information explicitly. Do not generate infrastructure files or change app code today.

Use these current file locations where they apply: docs/vps-plan.md and docs/checks/; the actual server/DNS settings, which are not source code. Create only files needed for today's task. Preserve existing paths unless a specific change is necessary. Return complete contents for each changed file, one labeled code block per path, and Mac run/test commands with working directories. Use VS Code GUI actions for Git operations. Do not use placeholders for facts already in the snapshot. If an account-specific value is missing, ask for that value before generating dependent configuration.

Our acceptance check is: Every account and operating task has an adult owner; no invented hostnames, secrets, or completed claims appear. Explain the expected observation and one relevant failure case. Do not report a pass until I provide actual output. Do not implement any later week's feature.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
6 Run the check and handle a failure here
1. Record the real VPS operating system, server address, DNS owner, domain, image registry, and adult operator. Mark missing inputs Blocked.
In a separate repository-root terminal, run the checks below. Run only against the isolated local test configuration when pytest is included. A stopped service or missing dependency must be resolved before recording a pass.
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
If a check fails: copy the exact command, the first useful error, what you expected, and what appeared into the same ChatGPT chat. Remove credentials. Then paste this ready-to-use repair request:
The check failed. The actual command, expected result, observed result, and first error are pasted immediately above. Use today's source snapshot and the files we changed in this chat. Identify the failing layer and give one small diagnostic first. Then give the smallest correction, complete contents for any changed file, and the exact Mac recheck command. Preserve working data and unrelated behavior. Do not regenerate the entire app or implement future tasks.
Save only the correction it returns, rerun the failed check, and repeat relevant earlier checks. If no failure occurred, skip the repair prompt. Keep the aide’s paper exercise moving while the teacher diagnoses.
7 Guide the student contribution
Students put four architecture cards in order and commit a plain-language deployment explanation.
Use the aide-supervised classroom Mac for the reference workflow so students do not need a separate advanced environment. Rotate driver and reviewer. The driver makes only the approved small edit or writes the evidence note; the reviewer compares the diff and runs the supplied check. Record each student’s name/pseudonym and contribution in docs/student-work/. The teacher controls account authentication and the final push.
If students use approved accounts on separate computers, their PR targets lesson/q4w1-mon until that class branch is merged. First, the teacher saves, reviews, stages, and clicks Commit, then Publish Branch (or … > Push). Students use Source Control > … > Fetch and the branch picker to open that published class branch; if already on it, use … > Pull. Students must receive today’s reviewed files before editing.
After the student contribution, repeat the visible acceptance check and run these commands before committing:
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
8 Record and save the reviewed result
In the same ChatGPT chat, paste a short factual observation: what you did, what appeared, and the actual build/test result. Then paste this request. Save the note as docs/lesson-notes/Q4W1-Mon.md and the revised board at its existing path.
Write docs/lesson-notes/Q4W1-Mon.md for Q4W1 Monday, task Map the deployment. Use only the observations I have just supplied to write today's lesson note. Include the task, the prompt improvement students made, paths changed, expected and actual results, pass/fail/not run, and the student contribution. Do not invent a successful check. Also provide the smallest change to docs/board.md that moves only verified work to Done; keep failures Blocked with a next action. Return complete contents for the named lesson-note file and revised board. Do not change application code.
In VS Code choose File > Save All. Click Source Control, then each file under Changes to inspect its diff. Click the + beside only each intended source, document, migration, or lockfile. Keep .env, virtual environments, node_modules, dist, dumps, and tokens out of Staged Changes. Then complete these steps:
1. In Source Control, click each file under Staged Changes to review the exact saved changes. If you edit again, save and stage that file again.
2. In the Message box, enter: Q4W1 Monday Map the deployment
3. Click Commit. If asked to stage everything, cancel and stage only the intended files with +.
4. Click Publish Branch for the first upload and choose origin if asked. If already published, click Source Control > … > Push. Confirm the branch is lesson/q4w1-mon and wait for the upload to finish.
5. Press Command+Shift+P and select GitHub Pull Requests: Create Pull Request. Set the base to the actual default branch and compare to lesson/q4w1-mon. Enter the lesson title and observed check results, then click Create.
6. Open Changes in Pull Request and review each diff with a student. In the PR Description, confirm required checks and approvals, then use the available Merge action and confirm. If blocked, keep the PR a draft/unmerged. If nothing changed, skip the empty commit and PR.
7. After GitHub confirms Merged, exit Review Mode if it is active. Click the bottom-left branch picker and select the default branch.
8. Click Source Control > … > Fetch, then … > Pull. Confirm the merged files appear in Explorer and there are no uncommitted changes or unexpected outgoing commits. Leave this updated default branch ready for the next lesson.
The next lesson begins from this merged checkpoint. If branch protection requires another approved reviewer, complete that review before proceeding. Do not force-push to bypass it.
