8. Click Source Control > … > Fetch, then … > Pull. Confirm the merged files appear in Explorer and there are no uncommitted changes or unexpected outgoing commits. Leave this updated default branch ready for the next lesson.
The next lesson begins from this merged checkpoint. If branch protection requires another approved reviewer, complete that review before proceeding. Do not force-push to bypass it.
9 Close the lesson
Ask: What should happen to a task when required access is missing?
Teacher answer: Mark it blocked and assign someone to resolve the dependency.
Use the student instructions on the next page to demonstrate the exact home example. Copy only the student portion into the assignment; keep the teacher key private. No separate prompt pack or appendix is required.
Q4W1 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML links https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_links.asp
Stop point: First Try it Yourself link example only. Change visible link text; keep its example destination.
Read this worked example first
<a href="https://example.com">Example page</a>
Example page is the visible text. The href value is the destination. Changing the text does not change the destination.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Open the first HTML link Try it Yourself example. Change only the visible link text to School timetable. Identify the destination separately.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. On paper match name to DNS, encrypted connection to HTTPS, and running server to VPS. No accounts or server access are required.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain backup in your own words.
2. Does a budget alert automatically shut down every Azure resource?
3. Explain VPS in your own words.
4. Should the database port be open to every internet user?
5. What should happen to a task when required access is missing?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Link text and destination are different. DNS maps names; HTTPS protects traffic; the VPS hosts services.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. A separate copy kept for recovery.
2. No. It is an alert; resource control needs a separate action.
3. A rented server managed by its owner.
4. No. Only the app should reach the private database.
5. Mark it blocked and assign someone to resolve the dependency.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q4W2 Weekly preparation
Deploy the app to its permanent VPS
Starting point: VPS, DNS, access, and a verified app image are ready.
End-of-week check: The app runs continuously at the permanent HTTPS address with private persistent storage.
Explain these ideas before the build
Deploying the same image helps isolate environment problems from code changes. The proxy handles HTTPS and forwards app requests. PostgreSQL uses a named volume and is not published to the internet. Restart policies and a running server keep the application available after class, but they do not replace backups or monitoring. A VPS redirect URI must be registered for Microsoft sign-in.
configuration: Settings that adapt the same code to an environment.
volume: Storage that can survive container replacement.
restart policy: A rule for restarting a service after it stops.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Confirm DNS propagation and access from Q4W1. The adult owner has the approved image registry credentials, database secret, and Entra registration access. Follow the VPS steps printed in this week during class.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Use a deployment checklist with named checks and real timestamps. Keep the previous working image available for rollback.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Request only the missing host configuration and supply actual image, hostname, ports, and environment variable names.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
