Q4W2 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML paragraphs https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_paragraphs.asp
Stop point: First Try it Yourself example only. Change the text inside one <p>...</p>; preserve the tags.
Read this worked example first
<p>Our class is planning a tool.</p>
Change planning to building. The displayed sentence changes; the paragraph tags stay the same.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. In the first paragraph editor, replace a sentence with The timetable is available at the school address. Run and observe.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Use a supplied paper checklist: page opens, login works, saved room remains. Explain why three checks tell us more than page opens alone.
2. Spend five minutes reviewing the example, 10–15 minutes on the task, and five minutes recording the result.
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
The checks cover availability, identity, and persistence separately. No home deployment task is required.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q4W3 Weekly preparation
Practice backup  recovery  and support
Starting point: The permanent VPS hosts the required current app.
End-of-week check: Scheduled off-host backups, a tested isolated restore, and an owner-run support routine exist.
Explain these ideas before the build
A persistent volume is not a backup. A useful backup must be separate from the machine it protects and must be restorable. Restore drills use an isolated database, never the live database. Monitoring needs someone who reads alerts and knows what to do. A rollback also needs migration compatibility; blindly running older code against a changed schema may fail.
backup: A separate copy used to recover data.
restore: Loading a backup into a database.
monitoring: Checking service health over time.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. The adult owner approves off-host storage, access, retention, and a monitoring contact. Select fictional demonstration data for the restore exercise. Never give production secrets to students.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Assign backup, alert, patch, and restore duties to adults. Record recovery evidence and an agreed retention period.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Ask for a plan with preconditions, isolated targets, checks, and stop conditions before requesting operational commands.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
Q4W3 Monday Schedule and protect backups
Target: A current off-host backup exists and the adult can verify schedule, retention, and failure handling.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q4w3-mon and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
Create and transfer a private VPS backup
Run on the VPS in its deploy directory. These commands create a custom-format dump inside db and copy it to a private directory outside the served files. Use a timestamped name so earlier backups are not overwritten.
mkdir -p "$HOME/class-scheduler-backups"
chmod 700 "$HOME/class-scheduler-backups"
course_backup="scheduler-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ).dump"
docker compose -f deploy/compose.vps.yaml --env-file .env exec -T db pg_dump -U scheduler -d scheduler -Fc -f "/tmp/$course_backup"
docker compose -f deploy/compose.vps.yaml --env-file .env cp "db:/tmp/$course_backup" "$HOME/class-scheduler-backups/$course_backup"
chmod 600 "$HOME/class-scheduler-backups/$course_backup"
Use the approved destination’s encrypted transfer method to copy the backup off the VPS. Do not attach the dump to ChatGPT. Give today’s prompt the destination type, non-secret path, schedule, retention decision, and failure-contact details so it can generate the exact scheduled job. Verify a real scheduled run; configuration alone is not evidence.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q4W3 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
Finder highlights the generated text file. Open and review it for accidental secrets before upload. In the Class Scheduler ChatGPT Project, start a chat named Q4W3 Monday. Attach this current text file. It contains actual source paths and the current commit, so you do not need to fill out a context form. Use this newest snapshot, not an older upload.
