Q4W5 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML forms https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_forms.asp
Stop point: First form example only. Identify its labels, inputs, and submit control; use fictional values.
Read this worked example first
<label>Name <input type="text" value="Alex"></label>
Name is the label; Alex is the current input value. The user can change the value without changing the label.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. Use the first form example to identify which control submits information. No sign-in or real personal data is needed.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Complete this paper rule table: Admin saves valid meeting = allow; Teacher saves meeting = deny; conflicting meeting = reject. Explain one result.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain user acceptance in your own words.
2. Does one user asking for a new feature make it a release requirement?
3. Explain regression in your own words.
4. Can hiding the Save button replace a server permission check?
5. Does a newer package version automatically prove the app still works?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Accept the stated outcomes and a reason linked to role or scheduling rules. No token or server work at home.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. Checking whether real users can complete intended tasks.
2. No. Compare it with the agreed scope and prioritize it separately.
3. A change that breaks previously working behavior.
4. No. The server must enforce permissions.
5. No. Run the appropriate checks after updating.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q4W6 Weekly preparation
Freeze scope and verify the release candidate
Starting point: Required repairs have been completed or remain explicit release blockers.
End-of-week check: A tagged release candidate satisfies every required feature and hosting check.
Explain these ideas before the build
A release candidate is a specific version, not a vague claim that the app is mostly done. A scope freeze protects time for verification. Traceability links each requirement to a real check and result. Azure must have its recorded teaching deployment evidence, and the permanent VPS must be currently available. Any missing required behavior remains a blocker; optional polish can wait.
release candidate: A version believed ready for final verification.
scope freeze: A decision to stop adding features.
traceability: Connecting a requirement to its task, change, and evidence.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Prepare the complete requirement matrix, test data, image tag, migration revision, backup record, and Azure evidence. Keep the remaining two weeks available for blockers and handoff.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Freeze new features. Use the acceptance matrix as the remaining task list and tag the tested commit after checks pass.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Ask AI to audit evidence against requirements and explicitly mark missing or contradictory evidence.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
Q4W6 Monday Audit requirement coverage
Target: All required rows have current evidence or a named blocker; no requirement is silently removed.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q4w6-mon and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q4W6 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
Finder highlights the generated text file. Open and review it for accidental secrets before upload. In the Class Scheduler ChatGPT Project, start a chat named Q4W6 Monday. Attach this current text file. It contains actual source paths and the current commit, so you do not need to fill out a context form. Use this newest snapshot, not an older upload.
3 Explain and choose the small task
Every required promise needs a matching demonstrated result. Ask students to predict this result: Does finding a function named publish prove publishing works?
Read the target above. Put only this task in Doing on docs/board.md once that file exists. Ask students to identify one useful constraint in the prompt and record their wording improvement without expanding scope.
4 Send the complete lesson prompt
We are doing Q4W6 Monday: Audit requirement coverage. I use a Mac and ChatGPT in a browser; I will copy your files into VS Code myself. Use the attached current source snapshot. At the start of this week: Required repairs have been completed or remain explicit release blockers. The actual snapshot takes precedence if a prior repair changed the file layout.

Today's request: Audit our acceptance matrix against these actual test records and current commit. For every required outcome, mark supported pass, failure, or missing evidence. Include manual scheduling, server conflicts, Microsoft roles, immutable publication, Azure evidence, and current VPS operation. Do not infer success from code presence. Produce only the evidence gaps and smallest required follow-up tasks.

Use these current file locations where they apply: docs/final-acceptance.md, docs/release-notes.md and docs/board.md; application files only for a demonstrated required blocker. Create only files needed for today's task. Preserve existing paths unless a specific change is necessary. Return complete contents for each changed file, one labeled code block per path, and Mac run/test commands with working directories. Use VS Code GUI actions for Git operations. Do not use placeholders for facts already in the snapshot. If an account-specific value is missing, ask for that value before generating dependent configuration.

Our acceptance check is: All required rows have current evidence or a named blocker; no requirement is silently removed. Explain the expected observation and one relevant failure case. Do not report a pass until I provide actual output. Do not implement any later week's feature.
5 Review then save each file
Send this review request in the same chat before copying the proposed code:
Review the file contents you just proposed against today's exact task and the attached current source. Name every affected path. Check imports, dependencies, commands, preserved behavior, and any unrelated or future-feature code. Give a corrected complete file only if needed; otherwise say no correction is needed. Identify which checks I must run on my Mac. Do not say they passed yet.
For each returned path, use VS Code Explorer. Create any missing parent folder with New Folder, then New File. For an existing file, open that exact file, select all its text with Command+A, paste the complete replacement, and save with Command+S. Copy only the code-block contents, not the surrounding backticks or explanation. Compare the changed paths with today’s task; reject unrelated future features.
6 Run the check and handle a failure here
1. For every required feature, locate a dated check result and version. A function name in source is not evidence of behavior.
In a separate repository-root terminal, run the checks below. Run only against the isolated local test configuration when pytest is included. A stopped service or missing dependency must be resolved before recording a pass.
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
If a check fails: copy the exact command, the first useful error, what you expected, and what appeared into the same ChatGPT chat. Remove credentials. Then paste this ready-to-use repair request:
The check failed. The actual command, expected result, observed result, and first error are pasted immediately above. Use today's source snapshot and the files we changed in this chat. Identify the failing layer and give one small diagnostic first. Then give the smallest correction, complete contents for any changed file, and the exact Mac recheck command. Preserve working data and unrelated behavior. Do not regenerate the entire app or implement future tasks.
Save only the correction it returns, rerun the failed check, and repeat relevant earlier checks. If no failure occurred, skip the repair prompt. Keep the aide’s paper exercise moving while the teacher diagnoses.
7 Guide the student contribution
Each student traces one requirement to its task, commit, and test result.
Use the aide-supervised classroom Mac for the reference workflow so students do not need a separate advanced environment. Rotate driver and reviewer. The driver makes only the approved small edit or writes the evidence note; the reviewer compares the diff and runs the supplied check. Record each student’s name/pseudonym and contribution in docs/student-work/. The teacher controls account authentication and the final push.
If students use approved accounts on separate computers, their PR targets lesson/q4w6-mon until that class branch is merged. First, the teacher saves, reviews, stages, and clicks Commit, then Publish Branch (or … > Push). Students use Source Control > … > Fetch and the branch picker to open that published class branch; if already on it, use … > Pull. Students must receive today’s reviewed files before editing.
After the student contribution, repeat the visible acceptance check and run these commands before committing:
npm --prefix frontend run build
cd backend
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
cd ..
8 Record and save the reviewed result
In the same ChatGPT chat, paste a short factual observation: what you did, what appeared, and the actual build/test result. Then paste this request. Save the note as docs/lesson-notes/Q4W6-Mon.md and the revised board at its existing path.
Write docs/lesson-notes/Q4W6-Mon.md for Q4W6 Monday, task Audit requirement coverage. Use only the observations I have just supplied to write today's lesson note. Include the task, the prompt improvement students made, paths changed, expected and actual results, pass/fail/not run, and the student contribution. Do not invent a successful check. Also provide the smallest change to docs/board.md that moves only verified work to Done; keep failures Blocked with a next action. Return complete contents for the named lesson-note file and revised board. Do not change application code.
In VS Code choose File > Save All. Click Source Control, then each file under Changes to inspect its diff. Click the + beside only each intended source, document, migration, or lockfile. Keep .env, virtual environments, node_modules, dist, dumps, and tokens out of Staged Changes. Then complete these steps:
1. In Source Control, click each file under Staged Changes to review the exact saved changes. If you edit again, save and stage that file again.
2. In the Message box, enter: Q4W6 Monday Audit requirement coverage
3. Click Commit. If asked to stage everything, cancel and stage only the intended files with +.
4. Click Publish Branch for the first upload and choose origin if asked. If already published, click Source Control > … > Push. Confirm the branch is lesson/q4w6-mon and wait for the upload to finish.
5. Press Command+Shift+P and select GitHub Pull Requests: Create Pull Request. Set the base to the actual default branch and compare to lesson/q4w6-mon. Enter the lesson title and observed check results, then click Create.
6. Open Changes in Pull Request and review each diff with a student. In the PR Description, confirm required checks and approvals, then use the available Merge action and confirm. If blocked, keep the PR a draft/unmerged. If nothing changed, skip the empty commit and PR.
7. After GitHub confirms Merged, exit Review Mode if it is active. Click the bottom-left branch picker and select the default branch.
8. Click Source Control > … > Fetch, then … > Pull. Confirm the merged files appear in Explorer and there are no uncommitted changes or unexpected outgoing commits. Leave this updated default branch ready for the next lesson.
The next lesson begins from this merged checkpoint. If branch protection requires another approved reviewer, complete that review before proceeding. Do not force-push to bypass it.
9 Close the lesson
Ask: Does finding a function named publish prove publishing works?
Teacher answer: No. A behavior check and observed result are required.
Q4W6 Tuesday Run the release acceptance suite
Target: All required checks pass for one identified candidate, or each failure remains an explicit blocker to fix this week/next week.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q4w6-tue and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
Run the complete product acceptance sequence here
Use known fictional records and actual IDs. Record the source commit, image tag, migration revision, environment, tester, date, expected result, actual result, and pass/fail. Repeat relevant checks after a fix. Do not count a not-run case as passed.
1. As Admin, create/edit a room, teacher, course, and section. Save a valid meeting, reload, move it, cancel removal, and then remove a separate test meeting. Unrelated records remain unchanged.
2. Place A in Room 101 Monday P1. Try B in that room/slot: reject. Try C taught by T1 in another room Monday P1: reject. Try duplicate A in another room same slot: reject. A at 24 seats in capacity 24 passes; A in capacity 18 rejects.
3. Try reducing a used room’s capacity or changing a section’s teacher/limit so existing meetings become invalid. Reject and preserve old values. Run isolated concurrent-write tests: exactly one conflicting save succeeds, and the other is rejected without corruption.
4. Test missing/invalid/expired/wrong-audience credentials; reject. Test unassigned identity; deny. Admin can write. Teacher cannot write, publish, read drafts, or request another Teacher’s timetable. Verify the direct API, not only hidden controls.
5. Publish valid Version 1. Cancel a new publication: no change. Edit a draft placement and label: Version 1 stays stable. Publish valid Version 2: Teacher sees the new approved version. Induce a test publication failure: old active version remains intact.
6. Sign in as each of two Teachers. Confirm server-filtered own meetings, correct day/period order, version/time, empty message where appropriate, and readable print preview with no unauthorized data.
7. Verify real Azure deployment evidence includes its HTTPS URL, image/version, roles, save/reload, conflict, publication, and persistence results. Azure resources can be retired only by the adult owner after required evidence and planned data transfer/retention are complete; the permanent VPS remains running.
8. On the permanent VPS, verify HTTPS and readiness from another device, then planned restart/reboot and persistence. Confirm off-host scheduled backup, successful isolated restore record, monitoring contact, billing/account owner, backup owner, and tested operator instructions.
Acceptance decision
All required rows pass by Q4W8. The adult signs the record and accepts continued operations. If any required row fails, it remains incomplete and receives immediate repair/retest time; it cannot be transferred to a post-year optional backlog.
