Q4W7 Thursday and Friday guided home work
Student instructions to copy into the assignment
Time: 20–30 minutes each day. Due the following Monday, with grace through Wednesday. Use the supplied example; do not install tools, use AI, access servers, or edit the class repository at home. If the site fails, use the paper option below for equal credit.
HTML lists https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_lists.asp
Stop point: Use only the ordered or unordered list example named in the task. Change item text, not the structure.
Read this worked example first
<ol><li>Choose</li><li>Save</li><li>Check</li></ol>
Each li is an item. Changing Save to Save draft changes that step’s words; the order stays the same.
Thursday steps
1. Spend five minutes reading the worked example. Open the named resource and its specified example. Ignore unrelated lessons or paid/account offers.
2. In the first unordered-list editor, replace the items with Plan, Build, and Check. Run the example.
3. Save a screenshot or draw a labeled sketch. Write: I changed or identified ___. I expected ___. I observed ___. Spend the final five minutes on this evidence and stop by 30 minutes.
Friday steps
1. Write three sentences using these starters: I helped by ___. We improved a prompt by ___. We checked the result by ___. Use class notes.
2. Spend 10–15 minutes on that example, then ten minutes on the five questions below; they replace extra practice.
1. Explain release candidate in your own words.
2. Why record the image or commit used for a test?
3. Explain buffer in your own words.
4. How can we tell whether a guide is clear?
5. Should a demonstration hide a failed required check?
Submit or use the paper route
Submit the screenshot/sketch and requested short answers. If you cannot use the site, copy the supplied example onto paper, circle the word/value to change, sketch the expected result, and answer the same questions. Stop after 30 minutes and report the access issue. No independent app construction is assigned.
Teacher only do not include in the student assignment
Credit specific, understandable examples. Students are not expected to explain advanced authentication or deployment code.
Score one point for each correct basic idea. Accept equivalent plain language. Answers:
1. A version believed ready for final verification.
2. So we know exactly which version was verified.
3. Reserved time for uncertainty and repairs.
4. Have another person follow it and observe where they get stuck.
5. No. Record and repair the failure rather than claiming success.
Record completion and feedback in the existing gradebook. Do not penalize the paper route or a device failure. Preserve the Monday due date, Wednesday grace period, and established late-work policy.
Q4W8 Weekly preparation
Complete  demonstrate  and hand over the product
Starting point: The finished candidate and operating guides are ready for final verification.
End-of-week check: All required acceptance checks pass; the permanent app and ownership handoff are complete.
Explain these ideas before the build
Completion means the agreed product works and someone owns its continued operation. The final demonstration is evidence, not a substitute for testing. Students should explain basic concepts, a prompt improvement, a project-management decision, and a verified contribution. Refinements may continue afterward, but required features, Azure teaching evidence, and permanent VPS operation are due this week.
acceptance: A recorded decision that agreed requirements are met.
maintenance: Ongoing care after release.
reflection: Explaining what was learned from evidence.
Prepare this week in order
1. Confirm the previous checkpoint works. Reserve Monday for final checks and urgent required repairs, Tuesday for demonstration, and Wednesday for handoff/assessment. Have the adult owner confirm ongoing hosting, backups, and support.
2. Prepare the fictional records or paper examples named above. Prepare account/tool access only; do not implement this week’s app changes before the live lesson.
3. Practice this project-management action with students: Close the project against the agreed scope. Transfer the nonblocking backlog and operations responsibility to the named adult owner.
4. Improve the prompt by focusing on this skill: Ask AI to summarize only verified outcomes and separate evidence from assumptions. Students check the summary for unsupported claims.
5. Keep the three lessons below in order. If a check fails, use that lesson’s repair prompt and preserve the working data. Record failed or not-run checks honestly.
Student record for the week
Each student keeps one note with their name/pseudonym, role, prediction, small change or review, observed result, and one concept in their own words. Use docs/student-work/ for class evidence. The teacher remains responsible for the application and all account credentials.
Q4W8 Monday Complete the final acceptance record
Target: Every required row is a supported pass by Q4W8; otherwise the record accurately states incomplete and class time stays on repair.
1 Prepare the current lesson
In VS Code, open the existing repository. Choose File > Save All, then click the Source Control icon in the left Activity Bar. Check both Changes and Staged Changes. Finish or preserve any earlier work on its own branch before switching. If resuming today’s existing branch, select it from the bottom-left branch picker and skip creating it again.
1. In Source Control, click … > Fetch.
2. Click the bottom-left branch name and select the actual default branch recorded during setup. In Graph, confirm it has no outgoing local commits; if it does, resolve that unexpected work before proceeding.
3. Click Source Control > … > Pull. Wait for completion. If VS Code reports conflicts, stop and resolve the indicated files with the aide before continuing.
4. Click the branch name again > Create new branch. Enter lesson/q4w8-mon and press Enter. Confirm that exact name appears in the status bar.
Run the complete product acceptance sequence here
Use known fictional records and actual IDs. Record the source commit, image tag, migration revision, environment, tester, date, expected result, actual result, and pass/fail. Repeat relevant checks after a fix. Do not count a not-run case as passed.
1. As Admin, create/edit a room, teacher, course, and section. Save a valid meeting, reload, move it, cancel removal, and then remove a separate test meeting. Unrelated records remain unchanged.
2. Place A in Room 101 Monday P1. Try B in that room/slot: reject. Try C taught by T1 in another room Monday P1: reject. Try duplicate A in another room same slot: reject. A at 24 seats in capacity 24 passes; A in capacity 18 rejects.
3. Try reducing a used room’s capacity or changing a section’s teacher/limit so existing meetings become invalid. Reject and preserve old values. Run isolated concurrent-write tests: exactly one conflicting save succeeds, and the other is rejected without corruption.
4. Test missing/invalid/expired/wrong-audience credentials; reject. Test unassigned identity; deny. Admin can write. Teacher cannot write, publish, read drafts, or request another Teacher’s timetable. Verify the direct API, not only hidden controls.
5. Publish valid Version 1. Cancel a new publication: no change. Edit a draft placement and label: Version 1 stays stable. Publish valid Version 2: Teacher sees the new approved version. Induce a test publication failure: old active version remains intact.
6. Sign in as each of two Teachers. Confirm server-filtered own meetings, correct day/period order, version/time, empty message where appropriate, and readable print preview with no unauthorized data.
7. Verify real Azure deployment evidence includes its HTTPS URL, image/version, roles, save/reload, conflict, publication, and persistence results. Azure resources can be retired only by the adult owner after required evidence and planned data transfer/retention are complete; the permanent VPS remains running.
8. On the permanent VPS, verify HTTPS and readiness from another device, then planned restart/reboot and persistence. Confirm off-host scheduled backup, successful isolated restore record, monitoring contact, billing/account owner, backup owner, and tested operator instructions.
Acceptance decision
All required rows pass by Q4W8. The adult signs the record and accepts continued operations. If any required row fails, it remains incomplete and receives immediate repair/retest time; it cannot be transferred to a post-year optional backlog.
2 Give ChatGPT the actual current files
bash scripts/classroom-context.sh Q4W8 Monday
open -R /tmp/class-scheduler-context.txt
